      "text": "### The Status of Moral Statements"
    },
    {
      "id": "05-ETHICS.md#008",
      "path": [
        "05-ETHICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:05-ethics, git-history:philosophy:05-ethics]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "concepts": "[ethics]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:ethics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ethics]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "05 - Ethics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nARE MORAL STATEMENTS TRUTH-APT?\n  Cognitivism: moral statements express beliefs; can be true or false\n  Non-cognitivism: moral statements express attitudes, commands, or emotions; not truth-apt\n    Emotivism (Ayer): \"Murder is wrong\" = \"Boo! Murder!\" — not a belief, an expression\n    Prescriptivism (Hare): moral statements are universalizable prescriptions\n    Expressivism (Blackburn, Gibbard): more sophisticated; moral attitudes expressed;\n      but can be subject to quasi-realist logical operations"
    },
    {
      "id": "05-ETHICS.md#009",
      "path": [
        "05-ETHICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:05-ethics, git-history:philosophy:05-ethics]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "concepts": "[ethics]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:ethics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ethics]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "05 - Ethics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "IF MORAL STATEMENTS ARE TRUTH-APT (cognitivism):\n  Moral realism: moral facts exist independently of any mind, culture, or attitude\n    Non-naturalism: moral facts are sui generis, irreducible to natural facts (Moore)\n    Naturalism: moral facts ARE natural facts (pain is bad = pain tends to cause functional impairment)\n    Cornell realism: moral properties are real but multiple-realizable natural properties\n  Error theory (Mackie): moral statements are truth-apt AND systematically false\n    There are no objective moral facts; all moral claims presuppose there are → all false\n    \"The world contains no values\" — moral discourse is a massive error\n  Constructivism (Rawls, Korsgaard): moral facts are constructed by ideal procedures or\n    by requirements of rational agency; not mind-independent but not merely subjective"
    },
    {
      "id": "05-ETHICS.md#010",
      "path": [
        "05-ETHICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:05-ethics, git-history:philosophy:05-ethics]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "concepts": "[ethics]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:ethics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ethics]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "05 - Ethics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "COMPANION IN GUILT ARGUMENT:\n  If you accept mathematical claims as objective (though abstract), why not moral claims?\n  Both resist naturalistic reduction; both seem to have modal force\n  Anti-realist about morality must explain why math gets to be objective and morality doesn't\n```"
    },
