      "text": "### Moral Epistemology"
    },
    {
      "id": "05-ETHICS.md#012",
      "path": [
        "05-ETHICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:05-ethics, git-history:philosophy:05-ethics]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "concepts": "[ethics]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:ethics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ethics]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "05 - Ethics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nHOW DO WE KNOW MORAL FACTS (if they exist)?\n  Intuitionism (Ross, Moore): direct apprehension of moral facts through intuition\n    Some moral truths are self-evident (similar to mathematical axioms)\n    Prima facie duties: keep promises; don't harm; tell truth; gratitude; etc.\n    Multiple non-reducible duties; real moral dilemmas when they conflict\n  Rationalism (Kant, Korsgaard): moral facts derivable from rational requirements\n    Categorical imperative is derivable from requirements of practical reason\n  Empiricism: moral facts discoverable empirically (if naturalistic reduction works)\n    Evolutionary debunking: our moral intuitions evolved for fitness not truth → unreliable?"
    },
    {
      "id": "05-ETHICS.md#013",
      "path": [
        "05-ETHICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:05-ethics, git-history:philosophy:05-ethics]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "concepts": "[ethics]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/05-ETHICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:ethics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ethics]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "05 - Ethics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "MORAL UNCERTAINTY:\n  We're uncertain about ethics just as about empirical facts\n  Appropriate response: be uncertain across multiple moral theories (MacAskill)\n  Moral parliament: weight moral theories by credence; average their verdicts\n  Risk-aversion under moral uncertainty: weight potentially catastrophic wrongs heavily\n  Implication for AI: align to morally uncertain humans; build in robustness to uncertainty\n```"
    },
