        "root_concepts": "[ethics]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "05 - Ethics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "OBJECTIONS TO UTILITARIANISM:\n  Justice: utility calculus can justify slavery if it maximizes aggregate welfare\n    Robert Nozick's utility monster: being that gets enormous utility from resources →\n    all resources should go to it by utilitarianism\n  Rights violations: harvest one person's organs to save five → utilitarian calculation says yes\n    Almost everyone rejects this: violates agent-relative constraints and deontological side-constraints\n  Demandingness: pure act utilitarianism demands giving until marginal utility equals recipient's\n    → most of your income should go to effective charities (Peter Singer's implication)\n  Aggregation: can we meaningfully sum utility across persons? Interpersonal comparison problem\n  Predictive difficulty: we can't know consequences; act utilitarianism impossible in practice"
    }
